Rename to <stage>-<hole>.t — ONE FILE PER TODO HOLE, not one per concept. `make lab` reports a
PASS/FAIL line per file, so file granularity is how a learner sees which hole they just finished.
State the RED condition in the header: which TODO(NN) hole must be filled before this goes green.

The paragraph above each case IS ITS NAME in the report (its first sentence, up to ~76 chars).
Write it as a claim that names the input and the expected outcome:

  `a == b` lexes as one `==`, not two `=` — maximal munch.
  The rest of the paragraph is detail for whoever reads the file; only the first sentence is
  shown by the runner:

  $ printf 'print(1 + 2)\n' > p.swift
  $ ./lab.exe --emit-sil p.swift | grep -c 'integer_literal'
  2

Keep each case inside its own stage: use the driver mode that stops there (`--emit-tokens` bails
right after lexing; `--typecheck` runs lex→parse→sema first, so a later stage's `failwith` masks
an earlier stage's diagnostic), and avoid syntax owned by another hole (the lexer's string cases
say `print("...")`, not `let s = "..."`, because `=` belongs to the operator hole).

Verify every golden by running the case against `solution/` — never hand-write expected output.

Prefer assertions that a WRONG implementation would fail: exact counts, exact diagnostics,
build-and-run with output compared against swiftc. Gotchas learned the hard way:
- `grep -c` exits 1 when the count is 0 — append `|| true` if 0 is the expected count.
- dune cram does NOT support `(re)`/`(glob)` patterns — goldens are literal text only.
- a binary killed by a signal makes the shell print a nondeterministic job message; wrap it:
  `$ sh -c './t; echo "exit=$?"' 2>/dev/null`
